sections = []
sections.append('''<section id="read"><span class="kicker">01 / Read the model</span><h2>Start with the voltage, then the role</h2><p class="lead">This guide explains the actual exported v4 Simulink model: what each subsystem does, what its components mean, and which details came from the supplied documents.</p><div class="legend"><div class="level red"><strong>230 kV</strong><span>Red · GSUT HV, GIS, line and grid</span></div><div class="level blue"><strong>22 kV</strong><span>Blue · generator and unit bus</span></div><div class="level green"><strong>6.6 kV</strong><span>Green · auxiliary switchboard</span></div><div class="level purple"><strong>110 V DC</strong><span>Purple · computed control/trip supply</span></div></div><p>Voltage colors answer “which electrical level is this?” The gallery’s <span class="badge source">PDF-SUPPORTED FACTS</span> and <span class="badge added">ADDED BY US</span> badges answer a different question: “where did this information or implementation come from?”</p><div class="steps"><div class="step"><b><i>1</i> Read the overview</b>Follow the main AC route left to right. Locate the UAT and GAT branches and the six presentation bus IDs.</div><div class="step"><b><i>2</i> Open the subsystem</b>Inspect its actual wiring, sensors, breakers, ports and named signals. Use the guide below to understand its purpose.</div><div class="step"><b><i>3</i> Find any block</b>Search the complete component reference by visible name, ID, area, block type or model path. Open its original PNG at full size.</div></div><div class="callout note"><strong>A snapshot records block design.</strong> Standalone Display blocks may be blank before Run initializes their runtime values. A blank display does not indicate a missing plant parameter. Read measurement units before comparing a displayed phase voltage with a bus’s nominal line-to-line voltage.</div>''' + figure('000','Complete model overview with voltage-coded electrical busbars') + '</section>')
sections.append('''<section id="power"><span class="kicker">02 / Electrical route</span><h2>Follow power from the machine to the grid</h2><p>In normal generation, mechanical input drives the generator at 22 kV. Power passes through the generator circuit breaker to the unit bus, then through the GSUT to 230 kV, Q0 and the GIS bus, the transmission line, and the PGCB receiving system. Auxiliary power branches from the unit bus through the UAT.</p>''' + flow + '''<p>The GAT gives an alternative auxiliary supply from 230 kV. It is out of service in the normal GAT-out study baseline. The model lets the user change its service state; the drawing alone is not evidence of a complete automatic transfer sequence.</p><div class="callout"><strong>6.9 kV and 6.6 kV describe different things.</strong> UAT/GAT secondary winding nameplate ratings are 6.9 kV. The modeled auxiliary switchboard and load nominal basis are 6.6 kV. Keep both labels so a reader can distinguish equipment ratio from board operating designation.</div><p>The 450 MW plant title is separate from the generator’s 458 MVA rating and the default 360 MW study dispatch. MW measures real power; MVA measures apparent power. Neither number should silently replace the other.</p></section>''')
busrows = [
 ('B_22','22 kV · blue','Generator → 52G / GCB','Machine-side junction before the generator breaker (22 kV Gen Bus).','034'),
 ('B_22_UNIT','22 kV · blue','After 52G → GSUT + UAT','Unit-side junction; the UAT branch is taken here (22 kV Unit Bus).','041'),
 ('B_230','230 kV · red','After Q0 → line + GAT','GIS switchyard junction bounded by incoming, line and GAT (230 kV GIS Bus).','358'),
 ('BGRID_230','230 kV · red','Receiving line → grid equivalent','PGCB-side receiving junction (Remote 230 kV Grid Bus).','119'),
 ('B6.6','6.6 kV · green','UAT / alternative GAT → auxiliary groups','Common auxiliary switchboard equivalent (6.6 kV MV Aux Bus).','009'),
 ('BDC110','110 V DC · purple','Charger + battery → control/trip demand','Computed inside Battery charger and DC bus; no separate physical DC conductor network.','069'),
]
rows=''.join('<tr><td>'+e(a)+'</td><td>'+e(b)+'</td><td>'+e(c)+'</td><td>'+e(d)+'<br>'+link(i)+'</td></tr>' for a,b,c,d,i in busrows)
sections.append('''<section id="busbars"><span class="kicker">03 / Busbar register</span><h2>Six named junctions, two representations</h2><p>A busbar is a common connection where circuits meet. B_22 to B6.6 are actual ideal three-phase pass-through connections inserted at the existing AC junctions: phase A stays separate from B and C. They do not add resistance, capacitance, protection or a new voltage source.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Level</th><th>Connection</th><th>Meaning and snapshot</th></tr></thead><tbody>''' + rows + '''</tbody></table></div><p><strong>B06 is different:</strong> its voltage and service status are calculated by the DC behavior model. Purple identifies that computed supply. The battery, charger, demands and resulting trip availability are modeled by signals and equations, not by a separate Simscape DC circuit.</p><p class="small">All six B-identifiers, their placement and voltage-color presentation were added by the project. They are navigation labels, not documented plant tag numbers such as 10BAT10 or 10BBT10.</p></section>''')

